\section{Anharmonic Mass Spectrum}

In this section we present the full anharmonic Kaluza--Klein (KK) mass spectrum
arising from the STME-RS-A framework. The quartic bulk term $\lambda_4$ modifies
the Sturm--Liouville eigenvalue problem and produces non-equidistant KK masses.
We derive the analytic mass formula, compare it with numerical solutions, and
identify collider-relevant mass ratios that serve as falsifiable observables.

\subsection{General Expression}

The KK masses follow from the eigenvalue equation
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}[f_n] = m_n^2 f_n,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}$ is the Sturm--Liouville operator defined in
Eq.~(\ref{eq:SL}). The anharmonic contribution modifies the effective potential
and shifts the eigenvalues.

The full mass spectrum is
\begin{equation}
m_n^2 =
4k^2
+ 2\sqrt{\lambda}\left(n+\tfrac{1}{2}\right)
+ \frac{12\lambda_4}{16\lambda^2}
\left( 6n^2 + 6n + 3 \right).
\label{eq:full_mass}
\end{equation}

The first term $4k^2$ is the curvature-induced baseline. The second term is the
harmonic RS contribution. The third term is the anharmonic shift.

