\subsection{Deviation from RS Equidistance}

In the RS model, the KK masses satisfy
\begin{equation}
m_n^{\text{RS}} \approx m_1^{\text{RS}} n,
\end{equation}
leading to the equidistant ratio
\begin{equation}
\frac{m_2^{\text{RS}}}{m_1^{\text{RS}}} = 2.
\end{equation}

In the STME-RS-A model, the anharmonic shift produces
\begin{equation}
\frac{m_2}{m_1} = 2 + \delta_{12},
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
\delta_{12} =
\frac{
\frac{12\lambda_4}{16\lambda^2}(6\cdot 2^2 + 6\cdot 2 + 3)
-
\frac{12\lambda_4}{16\lambda^2}(6\cdot 1^2 + 6\cdot 1 + 3)
}{
4k^2 + 2\sqrt{\lambda}\left(\tfrac{3}{2}\right)
}.
\end{equation}

This deviation is a key experimental observable.

